# Charness License 2.0

This file is the whole licence of Charness: when it is free, what you may do with the code, and the paid terms. It
covers the code of the commit it is in. A copy you received with a different licence file (an earlier release under MIT
or under the Charness License 1.0.0) keeps the licence that came with it.

The **licensor** is Sergei Solo (Georgian individual entrepreneur; registration details by e-mail on request). **You**
are the person or organization using these terms. Contact for everything in this licence: charnesshub@gmail.com.

Contents: Part 1, when Charness is free. Part 2, source available. Part 3, commercial terms. Part 4, general terms and
definitions.

## Part 1. When Charness is free

You may use Charness without paying in each of these cases (section 2.1 says what you may do with it).

1.1. **Personal use.** Hobby projects, study and independent projects that are not a business. Any paid work on your own
account, including freelancing or a one-person company, is business use and follows section 1.2. Work you do for an
employer is the employer's use, whoever owns the computer.

1.2. **Small organizations.** An organization group whose **R** (revenue plus funding, section 4.9) is at most USD
500,000 **and** whose **H** (group people, section 4.9) is at most 20. Both must hold; exactly USD 500,000 and 20 people
qualify. Users, agents, jobs, machines and instances are unlimited.

1.3. **Education and public benefit.** Teaching, student learning, and genuine academic or noncommercial research by
recognized educational or research institutions and individual researchers; and the own work of public or nonprofit
schools and universities, genuine charities and public authorities, whatever their budget, funding or headcount. A
commercial employer's research and development is ordinary group use, not free academic research merely because a
department calls itself a lab. A commercial operation of such an institution that uses Charness is free while its
external commercial revenue C (section 4.9) is at most USD 500,000 a year; above that it pays as an organization under
Part 3, counting that operation's own R and H. A for-profit spin-off or a government-owned trading company is an
ordinary organization from the start.

1.4. **Evaluation.** One period of less than 32 consecutive days per organization group, from the first time anyone in
the group runs Charness to evaluate it. Productive work is allowed only as part of evaluating. Pauses, other people or
other versions do not restart it.

1.5. **Growth.** An organization group that was free and exceeds a limit of section 1.2 at a month end may keep using
Charness for 90 days from that month end. By then it buys a licence (Part 3), falls back within the limits, or stops. A
second crossing within 12 months of the first does not restart the 90 days. A group already above the limits when it
starts gets no growth period. A free group that joins a larger group by a merger or acquisition has 90 days from
completion.

1.6. **Earlier MIT users.** A group that used an MIT-licensed Charness release before its first use of a release under
the Charness License may use releases under the Charness License for 90 days from that first use, once; it runs at the
same time as evaluation, not after it. MIT releases stay MIT.

1.7. **Measuring.** Check R and H at every month end, and at first use with the latest month-end figures (a new business
uses its actual figures since it started). No fees are ever due for use that was free under this Part.

1.8. **Work for clients.** A provider that runs Charness only on its own systems to produce work for a client uses it
under its own free use or licence, whatever the client's size. When Charness runs on systems the client owns, rents,
pays for or controls, or when the client's people administer it or make its agents act, it is the client's use, under
the client's own free use or licence (which the provider may buy for it).

## Part 2. Source available

Charness is **source available**, not open source: the code is open to read, and you may use it under these terms.

2.1. **Use and change.** For a use that is free under Part 1 or paid under Part 3, the licensor grants you a copyright
licence to run, copy and modify Charness, and a licence under the licensor's patents that Charness would otherwise
infringe.

2.2. **Sharing.** You may share copies of Charness and of your changes without charge, including public mirrors and
forks, if every copy keeps this file and the `Required Notice:` lines. Recipients get no broader rights than these terms
and must qualify for their own use. You may install or maintain Charness for a client under the client's own free use or
licence (section 1.8), and charge for that service, but not for the software. Selling copies, embedding Charness in a
product, or offering it as a hosted service needs section 3.4 or 3.5, even at no charge.

2.3. **Derived code.** Code written on the basis of Charness code or commits, taking over its approaches and solutions,
is a work based on Charness and is subject to this licence like Charness itself, whoever or whatever wrote it, including
such code an AI model wrote with Charness code or commits in its context.

2.4. **Output.** Work you only produce by running Charness, such as your own code, documents or other deliverables that
contain no Charness code and are not derived under section 2.3, is yours. The licensor claims no ownership or royalty in
it. The prompts, skills and tool descriptions Charness gives an agent while it works on your project do not make that
work derived; section 2.3 covers code that takes over Charness's own approaches and solutions.

2.5. **AI training.** Training or fine-tuning a machine-learning model on Charness code or commits is allowed for
payment under section 3.3. Part 1 does not make free the training of a model that is used, sold or offered under section
3.3.

2.6. **Earlier MIT code.** Code and commits published under the MIT licence (every release before 4.0) stay under MIT;
sections 2.3, 2.5 and 3.3 do not reach code built on them or a model trained on them alone.

2.7. **Names.** These terms grant no right to the name Charness or the licensor's marks beyond the required notices and
uses the law allows without permission. Section 3.6(a) adds one limited permission.

## Part 3. Commercial terms

Any use that Part 1 does not make free needs a paid licence under this Part. Prices are in US dollars, per organization
group, excluding taxes.

3.1. **Organization licence.** One yearly price for the whole organization group, by its size: find the band of R and
the band of H separately and pay the higher of the two. An upper revenue boundary belongs to the lower band. The licence
covers the group's internal use with unlimited users, agents, jobs, machines and instances, including unattended runs
and contractors acting for the group; it is never priced per user or per seat.

| Band | R: revenue plus funding, last 12 months | H: group people | Price per year |
|---|---|---|---:|
| Free | up to $500,000 | up to 20 | $0 |
| Small | over $500,000, up to $2,000,000 | 21–50 | $500 |
| Growing | over $2,000,000, up to $10,000,000 | 51–250 | $2,000 |
| Established | over $10,000,000, up to $50,000,000 | 251–1,000 | $6,000 |
| Large | over $50,000,000, up to $250,000,000 | 1,001–5,000 | $15,000 |
| Very large | over $250,000,000, up to $1,000,000,000 | 5,001–10,000 | $30,000 |
| Global | over $1,000,000,000 | over 10,000 | $60,000 |

3.2. **Term and growth.** A licence lasts one year, or two or three years when prepaid (section 3.6(b)), from the start
date on the invoice. It covers every version released before or during it. It is yearly, not perpetual: without renewal,
paid use ends (an independent free right under Part 1 or an MIT release still applies; you keep your copies and your
output). Ordinary growth during a paid year costs nothing extra; the next renewal uses the latest figures and may move
up or down a band, or back to free. A merger or acquisition needs a reassessment of the combined group within 90 days of
completion and the positive difference, prorated for the rest of the term, with overlapping fees already paid credited.
A wrong initial figure is corrected to the price that was due.

3.3. **AI training licence.** An organization group that uses, sells or offers to others a model trained or fine-tuned
on Charness code or commits — by its members, by anyone for it, or by whoever it got the model's weights from — pays the
price of its group's band in section 3.1 for every licence year in which it does so, whatever Part 1 would otherwise
allow. A licence year is 12 months from the invoice start date; the first begins when the model is first used, sold or
offered, and each year's band follows the latest month-end figures before that year starts. A group in the Free band
pays $0 by that table and sends no order. Training done by a group's people, its contractors or anyone it engages for it
is that group's training. An individual who trains such a model on their own account, not for an employer or client,
counts, for this section, as an organization group of its own, with its own R and H. This fee is on top of the
organization licence for using Charness. The receipt (or the paid invoice) is the training licence; keep it as proof.

3.4. **Embedded products (OEM).** A product that includes or runs Charness, or supplies its agent orchestration as a
feature, on premises or hosted, needs a signed OEM addendum before it ships, even at $0. The yearly OEM amount follows
P, the yearly revenue of all of the group's products that embed Charness:

| Embedded-product revenue P | OEM amount per year |
|---|---:|
| up to $500,000 | $0 |
| over $500,000, up to $2,000,000 | $2,000 |
| over $2,000,000, up to $10,000,000 | $6,000 |
| over $10,000,000, up to $50,000,000 | $15,000 |
| over $50,000,000, up to $250,000,000 | $30,000 |
| over $250,000,000 | $60,000 |

The supplier pays the **greater** of its organization licence fee (section 3.1) and the OEM amount, never their sum. Its
customers may run the embedded component inside that product, only for the product-specific tasks the OEM addendum
names, without a Charness fee of their own; they may never extract it, use it for other automation or offer it as a
general-purpose platform. That right survives for on-premises versions they lawfully received, but gives no updates;
hosted customers need a current supplier agreement. A no-fee OEM term also lasts one year and is renewed in writing.

3.5. **Hosting and resale.** Selling or offering substantially Charness itself as a hosted, managed, rebranded or resold
service, even through a new interface, API or AI intermediary, needs a separate signed agreement. Each client then needs
its own free use or licence, which the provider may buy for it. Write to charnesshub@gmail.com.

3.6. **Discounts.** Only these two, and only when the invoice states them. They apply to the organization licence fee
and to OEM amounts, never to $0 or to the training fee.

(a) **Mark, 20 %.** Throughout the term the group shows the "Powered by Charness" mark, with the link the licensor
supplies, in a product's customer-visible interface or on its public website, reachable without login. For that purpose
and that term the licensor grants a non-exclusive, non-transferable permission to show the mark, and no other use of the
name: it may not suggest endorsement or partnership, and never replaces the required notices. The licensor may ask the
group to change or remove the mark at any time; complying keeps the discount. If the mark is gone and not restored
within 30 days after notice, the discount for the rest of the term becomes payable, prorated by days.

(b) **Prepayment, 15 %.** The group pays a two- or three-year term in advance. Prices stay fixed for the term; the band
is still checked at each anniversary, and a higher one costs the discounted difference for the remaining years. A lower
one gives no refund.

Both together multiply: the price times 0.80 times 0.85, that is 68 % of the price. Examples: $500 becomes $400 with the
mark, $425 prepaid, $340 with both; $6,000 becomes $4,800, $5,100 or $4,080 a year ($12,240 for three prepaid years).

3.7. **How to buy.** A person or an AI agent acting for the organization can follow these steps. An agent may buy on its
own when the organization lets it spend; the organization is then the buyer and is bound. An order is the organization's
statement that its sender may bind it and the group it names.

Checkout: none yet (step 3 is by e-mail).

1. Check Part 1 and section 3.3. If your use is free under Part 1 and you train no model on Charness, or your group is
   in the Free band, you need nothing and send no order.
2. Work out the price: your band (section 3.1) from the latest month-end figures, and any discount (section 3.6).
3. Send an order to charnesshub@gmail.com with: the organization's legal name and country, the group it covers, its R
   and H bands, the term (1, 2 or 3 years), the discounts wanted, and the e-mail for the invoice. For the AI training
   licence (section 3.3), say so.
4. Pay the invoice you get back.
5. Keep the paid invoice or its receipt: either is your licence and its proof. There is no key and nothing to install.

3.8. **Refunds.** A full refund within 30 days after any payment, without a reason, once per group in any 12 months;
after 30 days, prepaid years are not refunded pro rata. How to ask:
https://olosegres.github.io/charness/commercial/refunds.html. Paid rights end on refund.

3.9. **Not included.** The fee does not include hosting, model or API costs, installation, consulting, training, support
or any promise of future releases. Third-party services (Anthropic, OpenAI, Telegram, Jira and others) are under their
own terms; you supply and pay for your own accounts and follow those terms. Anyone may sell services around Charness
without a royalty on them.

3.10. **Referrals.** A partner with a separate signed partner agreement earns 20 % of the fees actually collected from a
new customer it referred, for that customer's first 12 months, excluding taxes, refunds and chargebacks.

## Part 4. General terms and definitions

4.1. **Acceptance.** To get any right under these terms you must agree to them, as obligations and as conditions of your
rights.

4.2. **Verification.** Organizations, including free ones, keep records that support their R, H, C or P figures for
three years. There are no licence keys, activation, compliance telemetry or remote shutdown. On a credible discrepancy
the licensor may first ask for a written certification by an officer or accountant, with 30 days to answer. Only a
discrepancy still unresolved after that allows a confidential check by an independent professional, at most once in 12
months, on 30 days' notice, limited to the records that decide eligibility and fees; never code, prompts, credentials,
customer data or production systems. The licensor pays for the check unless the underpayment exceeds both 10 % of the
fees due for the reviewed period and USD 1,000; then the organization pays the reasonable documented cost of the check,
capped at the underpayment recovered. Only actual unpaid fees are due, never a penalty multiple.

4.3. **Violations.** The first time you are told in writing that you broke these terms, your rights continue if you
comply, and take practical steps to fix past violations, within 32 days. Otherwise your rights end. A verification
request is not a violation, and the end of a paid term is not a breach. Payment of overdue fees does not create a
perpetual licence.

4.4. **Patent defense.** If you, or an organization you work for or any member of its group, claim in writing that
Charness infringes a patent, your patent licence ends at once.

4.5. **No other rights.** You may not sublicense or transfer your rights, except that a paid licence moves with the
business it belongs to, on written notice. The licensor may transfer these terms and paid licences to a successor of its
Charness business, on notice. These terms imply no other licences. Fair-use rights are not limited.

4.6. **No warranty, limited liability.** ***As far as the law allows, Charness comes as is, without any warranty or
condition, express or implied, including merchantability, fitness for a particular purpose and non-infringement.*** The
licensor is not liable for indirect or consequential loss, or loss of profit, data or business, including loss caused by
an agent; its total liability is limited to the fees you paid in the 12 months before the claim. Nothing limits
liability that the law does not allow to be limited.

4.7. **Law.** Georgian law governs these terms; the UN Convention on Contracts for the International Sale of Goods does
not apply. The courts of Tbilisi decide disputes, unless mandatory law says otherwise. If a merchant of record sells a
licence, its buyer terms govern that purchase and these terms the software rights.

4.8. **Changes and versions.** The licensor may publish new terms; they apply to code published with them, never
silently to a licence already paid. Any change of these terms gets a new version number, and the text of each version
stays unchanged at https://github.com/olosegres/charness/blob/license-<version>/LICENSE (this one: license-2.0). A
renewal at a new price needs 30 days' notice.

4.9. **Definitions.**

- **Charness:** the software, documentation and agent skills the licensor publishes under this licence.
- **Organization:** a company, sole proprietorship, partnership or any group working together, incorporated or not.
- **Organization group (group):** the organization together with every organization that controls it, is controlled by
  it or is under common control with it, worldwide. **Control** is more than 50 % of the votes or the actual power to
  direct management, held directly, indirectly or by persons acting together. Reorganizations, split invoices and new
  subsidiaries do not reset the figures.
- **R (revenue plus funding):** the group's consolidated gross revenue over the last 12 completed months, plus the
  investment and commercial-grant funding it received in them: equity, capital contributions, convertible notes, SAFEs,
  and loans from owners or investors that are not on ordinary market terms. Ordinary bank loans do not count. Count
  revenue by consistent accounting, after sales taxes and refunds, before costs; count each receipt once. Convert other
  currencies at monthly central-bank average rates.
- **H (group people):** the group's current employees (full- or part-time, including those on leave), working founders,
  owners and directors, and unpaid people working regularly; plus assigned contractors and agency personnel currently
  engaged who worked for the group on more than 30 days in the last 12 months. Count identifiable assigned workers in
  any business function, not a supplier's whole staff merely because the group buys its services. Count every person
  once. Customers, students, passive investors, AI agents, bots and machines are not people for H.
- **C (external commercial revenue):** the revenue of the commercial operation of an institution of section 1.3 that
  uses Charness, over the last 12 completed months, excluding its core-purpose grants and donations. Connected
  commercial operations under common institutional control are combined; an operation is not split into small
  departments to stay under the limit.
- **P (embedded-product revenue):** the revenue of all the group's products that embed Charness, over the last 12
  completed months, counting the whole product, after sales taxes and refunds. Include mandatory bundled charges, and
  split a bundle by standalone selling prices, never by a token product price that hides compulsory setup or services.
